Credential Access – ntds.dit Investigation Report
Credential Access – ntds.dit
Investigation

Since the case is related to ntds.dit, the investigation was started by searching for the keyword ntds.dit.

The investigation identified activity involving the ntds.dit file, which is the Active Directory database on a Domain Controller and contains information related to domain accounts and credentials.

Observed File Access Permissions

The following permissions were observed for the ntds.dit object:

Accesses:

READ_CONTROL
SYNCHRONIZE
ReadData (or ListDirectory)
ReadEA
ReadAttributes

Access Reasons:

READ_CONTROL: Granted by Ownership
SYNCHRONIZE: Granted by D:(A;ID;FA;;;BA)
ReadData (or ListDirectory): Granted by D:(A;ID;FA;;;BA)
ReadEA: Granted by D:(A;ID;FA;;;BA)
ReadAttributes: Granted by D:(A;ID;FA;;;BA)

Additional Information:

Access Mask: 0x120089
Privileges Used for Access Check: -
Restricted SID Count: 0
ACL Entry Explanation

The security descriptor D:(A;ID;FA;;;BA) can be interpreted as:

Part	Meaning
D:	DACL — Discretionary Access Control List
A	Allow
ID	Inherited
FA	Full Access
BA	Built-in Administrators group

Therefore, the relevant permissions were allowed through an inherited ACL entry granting Full Access to the Built-in Administrators group.

This indicates that the observed read-related permissions were permitted by the existing access-control configuration.

Duplicate ntds.dit File Observed

During the investigation, a duplicate/copy of the ntds.dit file was observed.


The creation of a copy of ntds.dit is an important observation because access to this file can expose sensitive Active Directory credential information. Therefore, the process responsible for creating or accessing the copy was investigated further.

Process Investigation

The SHA/hash of the observed file was investigated and was associated with a Google Android testing file.


The file identified as:

android-cts-7.1_r6-linux_x86-arm.zip

was observed in the activity and was executed through cmd.exe.


Further investigation showed that the activity was associated with the Android Compatibility Test Suite (CTS) testing content.

Overall Activity

The investigation identified access to ntds.dit and the creation of a duplicate/copy of the file. Because ntds.dit contains sensitive Active Directory information, this behavior initially appeared relevant to the Credential Access technique.

However, the associated file and execution activity were identified as being related to Google Android testing content. The observed activity therefore appears consistent with a testing activity rather than confirmed malicious credential theft, based on the available evidence.

Key Findings
ntds.dit was accessed.
Read-related permissions were observed.
The relevant ACL indicated inherited Full Access for the Built-in Administrators group.
A duplicate/copy of ntds.dit was observed.
The associated activity involved android-cts-7.1_r6-linux_x86-arm.zip.
The file was executed through cmd.exe.
The observed file/hash was associated with Google Android testing activity.
No additional evidence of credential extraction or subsequent malicious activity was identified in the observed data.
Conclusion

The case demonstrates how access to ntds.dit can initially indicate potential credential-access activity and why the responsible process and surrounding events must be investigated before classifying the activity as malicious.

Based on the available evidence, the observed activity is consistent with testing activity involving the Android Compatibility Test Suite, rather than confirmed credential theft.

One important correction: I would avoid writing “admin has full access to ntds.dit” as though an administrator definitely performed the access. The more accurate statement is that the ACL contains an inherited Allow entry granting Full Access to the Built-in Administrators group.

Also, your strongest investigation point is not the ACL itself—it is the chain:

ntds.dit → duplicate/copy created → associated file identified → cmd.exe execution → Android CTS/testing context → no additional malicious activity observed.

That makes the case much stronger as a threat-hunting investigation because you show how you moved from a potentially suspicious credential-access indicator to contextualizing the activity.
